This formula defines the total Lagrangian density (
 $ \mathcal{L} $
 ) of a physical system, typically in the context of classical or quantum field theory. It states that the total Lagrangian density is composed of two primary parts: a "free" part (
 $ \mathcal{L}_{free} $
 ), which describes the dynamics of non-interacting fields, and an "interaction" part (
 $ \mathcal{L}_{int} $
 ), which accounts for how these fields interact with each other. The Lagrangian density is a fundamental quantity whose integral over spacetime yields the action, from which the equations of motion for the fields are derived using the principle of least action.
